#Escape Characters in Python 

Escape characters are special characters that start with a backslash (\). They are used to represent characters that are difficult or impossible to type directly.
Example 1: New Line (\n)
print("Hello\nPythoon")

Output

Hello
Python
Example 2: Tab (\t)
print("Name\tAge")
print("Ujjawal\t21")

Output

Name    Age
Ujjawal 21
Example 3: Quotes
print("He said \"Hello\"")
print('It\'s raining')

Output

He said "Hello"
It's raining
Index Values in Python

An index is the position of a character in a string (or an element in a list).

Python uses zero-based indexing, which means counting starts from 0.

Positive Index
name = "Python"
Character	P	y	t	h	o	n
Index	0	1	2	3	4	5

Example:

name = "Python"

print(name[0])
print(name[3])
print(name[5])

Output

P
h
n
Negative Index

Negative indexing starts from the end of the string.

Character	P	y	t	h	o	n
Negative Index	-6	-5	-4	-3	-2	-1

Example:

name = "Python"

print(name[-1])
print(name[-3])

Output

n
h
Indexing Example
name = "Ujjawal"

print(name[0])
print(name[2])
print(name[-1])

Output

U
j
l
String Slicing

Syntax:

string[start : stop : step]
start → Starting index (included)
stop → Ending index (not included)
step → Number of positions to move
Example
name = "Python"

print(name[0:4])
print(name[2:6])
print(name[::2])
print(name[::-1])

Output

Pyth
thon
Pto
nohtyP
Easy Way to Remember
Escape Characters
\n → N = New line
\t → T = Tab
\\ → Print \
\" → Print ".
\' → Print '
Index Values
String:   P   y   t   h   o   n
Positive: 0   1   2   3   4   5
Negative:-6 -5  -4  -3  -2  -1
